Registers can also overflow into slower storage. Kernel design therefore balances fewer external reads against enough on-chip capacity and parallel work.

5. What this changes across the stack

Memory reuse explains why HBM bandwidth alone does not determine accelerator performance. Two chips with similar HBM can produce different results because their caches, on-chip storage, scheduling and software use that bandwidth differently.

It also gives optimized libraries and kernels economic value. Better software can serve more work from the same hardware, lowering accelerator time and energy per useful output.

If a model or batch cannot fit in one accelerator's memory, data may cross a scale-up link or cluster network. Local reuse can reduce pressure on both.

The idea will return when we compare GPUs with custom accelerators. A custom design may arrange memory around a narrower workload, while a GPU preserves more flexibility. The winner depends on achieved workload performance and total system cost, not one peak specification.

Quick check — answer before reading on

Assume an illustrative memory system delivers 1 terabyte per second. Kernel A performs 0.5 operation per byte moved, while tiled Kernel B performs 32 operations per byte. Before considering the compute ceiling, what memory-limited rates do they imply?

Answer: Kernel A is limited to about 0.5 trillion operations per second, or 0.5 TOPS. Kernel B is limited to about 32 TOPS. Reuse raised the memory-side ceiling by 64 times without changing external bandwidth.

Kernel B is not guaranteed to run 64 times faster. It may hit the arithmetic ceiling, use too many registers or lose occupancy. The calculation shows a memory limit, not achieved speed.

Remember these three things

- HBM provides capacity and broad bandwidth, while caches, shared memory and registers keep active data closer to the math.

- Arithmetic intensity measures useful calculation per byte moved; reuse raises it.

- The best memory strategy balances fewer external reads against limited on-chip storage and enough parallel work.

